Q1. Creating a branch

Bash
git branch feature/add-sponsors
Q2. Switching branches

Bash
git switch feature/add-sponsors
(Older alternative: git checkout feature/add-sponsors)

Q3. Create and switch in one command

Bash
git switch -c fix/typo-in-overview
(Older alternative: git checkout -b fix/typo-in-overview)

Intermediate
Q4. The beginner mistake

They should always run git status (or git branch) first to see which branch they are currently on before editing any files.

Q5. Branch naming convention

A new feature to add user authentication: feature/user-authentication (or feat/add-user-auth)

A fix for a spelling error in the README: fix/readme-spelling-error (or fix/typo-in-readme)

An experiment with a new layout design: experiment/new-layout-design (or exp/new-layout)

Hard
Q6. Understanding isolation

What happens to the new file on main: The new file disappears from your working directory because it was only committed on feature/new-logo, leaving main completely unchanged.

Why this matters: It keeps main clean, stable, and free of unfinished or experimental code until your changes are tested and ready to merge.

How to see the file again: Switch back to the feature branch using git switch feature/new-logo (or merge feature/new-logo into main).

Q7. Build a branch workflow

Create and switch to a new branch: git switch -c feature/new-section (or git checkout -b feature/new-section)

After making your changes and committing, switch back with: git switch main (or git checkout main)

The command to see all branches: git branch
